    <!-- ======================================================
         PART 1: MODES OF CONVERGENCE
         ====================================================== -->
    <h2>Part 1: Modes of Convergence in Probability Theory</h2>

    <h3>1.1 The Four Main Modes of Convergence</h3>
    <p>
      Unlike deterministic real sequences, a sequence of random variables <em>{X<sub>n</sub>}</em> defined on a probability space <em>(&Omega;, F, P)</em> can approach a limiting random variable <em>X</em> in several distinct ways. Each mode captures a different notion of "closeness" between <em>X<sub>n</sub></em> and <em>X</em> as <em>n &rarr; &infin;</em>.
    </p>

    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Mode of Convergence</th>
          <th>Formal Definition (as <em>n &rarr; &infin;</em>)</th>
          <th>Intuition &amp; Key Role in Statistics</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><strong class="highlight">Almost Sure (a.s.)</strong><br><em>X<sub>n</sub> &rarr;<sup>a.s.</sup> X</em></td>
          <td><em>P({&omega; &isin; &Omega; : lim<sub>n&rarr;&infin;</sub> X<sub>n</sub>(&omega;) = X(&omega;)}) = 1</em></td>
          <td>Pointwise convergence of individual sample trajectories with probability 1. Foundation of the <strong class="highlight">Strong Law of Large Numbers (SLLN)</strong> and strong consistency of estimators.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong class="highlight">Mean-Square (L<sup>2</sup>)</strong><br><em>X<sub>n</sub> &rarr;<sup>L&sup2;</sup> X</em></td>
          <td><em>lim<sub>n&rarr;&infin;</sub> E[(X<sub>n</sub> &minus; X)<sup>2</sup>] = 0</em></td>
          <td>The average squared error vanishes. Directly corresponds to <strong class="highlight">MSE consistency</strong> of estimators (<em>MSE(&theta;&#770;<sub>n</sub>) = Bias<sup>2</sup> + Var &rarr; 0</em>) and proves the WLLN under finite variance.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong class="highlight">In Probability</strong><br><em>X<sub>n</sub> &rarr;<sup>p</sup> X</em></td>
          <td>For every <em>&epsilon; &gt; 0</em>:<br><em>lim<sub>n&rarr;&infin;</sub> P(|X<sub>n</sub> &minus; X| &gt; &epsilon;) = 0</em></td>
          <td>The probability of an unusually large deviation at step <em>n</em> shrinks to 0 (though rare spikes may still occur infinitely often along a trajectory). Core of the <strong class="highlight">Weak Law of Large Numbers (WLLN)</strong> and <strong class="highlight">weak consistency</strong>.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong class="highlight">In Distribution (Weak)</strong><br><em>X<sub>n</sub> &rarr;<sup>d</sup> X</em></td>
          <td><em>lim<sub>n&rarr;&infin;</sub> F<sub>X<sub>n</sub></sub>(x) = F<sub>X</sub>(x)</em><br>at every continuity point <em>x</em> of <em>F<sub>X</sub></em></td>
          <td>Only the cumulative distribution functions (CDFs) converge; <em>X<sub>n</sub></em> and <em>X</em> need not even share the same probability space. Core of the <strong class="highlight">Central Limit Theorem (CLT)</strong> and asymptotic confidence intervals.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3>1.2 Implication Hierarchy</h3>
    <p>
      These four modes form a strict logical hierarchy. Both almost sure convergence and <em>L<sup>2</sup></em> convergence imply convergence in probability (via Fatou's Lemma / continuity of measure and Markov's / Chebyshev's inequality, respectively), and convergence in probability implies convergence in distribution. However, neither almost sure convergence nor <em>L<sup>2</sup></em> convergence implies the other in general:
    </p>

    <div class="diagram-box">   Almost Sure (a.s.) ───┐
                         ├──►  In Probability (p)  ───►  In Distribution (d)
   Mean-Square (L²)  ────┘            ▲
                                      │ (Only if limit X = c is a constant)
                                      └───────────────────────────────────┘</div>

    <ul>
      <li><strong class="highlight">Why a.s. does not imply L<sup>2</sup>:</strong> A sequence can converge to 0 on every path while taking an increasingly huge value on a vanishingly small event (e.g., <em>X<sub>n</sub> = n</em> with probability <em>1/n<sup>2</sup></em>, 0 otherwise: Borel-Cantelli gives <em>X<sub>n</sub> &rarr;<sup>a.s.</sup> 0</em>, yet <em>E[X<sub>n</sub><sup>2</sup>] = 1 &ne; 0</em>).</li>
      <li><strong class="highlight">Why L<sup>2</sup> does not imply a.s.:</strong> The "typewriter sequence" (a sliding indicator window of shrinking width <em>1/k</em> sweeping endlessly over [0, 1]) has <em>E[X<sub>n</sub><sup>2</sup>] &rarr; 0</em>, yet every individual point <em>&omega;</em> oscillates between 0 and 1 infinitely often.</li>
      <li><strong class="highlight">Key Statistical Companion Theorems:</strong> The <strong class="highlight">Continuous Mapping Theorem</strong> preserves a.s., in-probability, and in-distribution convergence through continuous functions <em>g(&middot;)</em>. <strong class="highlight">Slutsky's Theorem</strong> combines convergence in distribution with convergence in probability to a constant (e.g., replacing unknown <em>&sigma;</em> with consistent sample standard deviation <em>S<sub>n</sub></em> in a t-statistic so <em>&radic;n (X&#772;<sub>n</sub> &minus; &mu;) / S<sub>n</sub> &rarr;<sup>d</sup> N(0, 1)</em>).</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>1.3 Roles in the LLN and CLT</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong class="highlight">Weak Law of Large Numbers (WLLN):</strong> For i.i.d. random variables with mean <em>&mu;</em>, the sample mean satisfies <em>X&#772;<sub>n</sub> &rarr;<sup>p</sup> &mu;</em>. When variance <em>&sigma;<sup>2</sup> &lt; &infin;</em>, <em>E[(X&#772;<sub>n</sub> &minus; &mu;)<sup>2</sup>] = &sigma;<sup>2</sup>/n &rarr; 0</em>, giving <em>L<sup>2</sup></em> convergence first and convergence in probability immediately via Chebyshev's inequality.</li>
      <li><strong class="highlight">Strong Law of Large Numbers (SLLN):</strong> Strengthens the conclusion to <em>X&#772;<sub>n</sub> &rarr;<sup>a.s.</sup> &mu;</em> (Kolmogorov's SLLN requires only <em>E[|X<sub>1</sub>|] &lt; &infin;</em>). Rather than guaranteeing proximity at a single large <em>n</em>, it guarantees that almost every infinite sequence of observations settles permanently at <em>&mu;</em>.</li>
      <li><strong class="highlight">Central Limit Theorem (CLT):</strong> Whereas the LLN collapses the distribution of <em>X&#772;<sub>n</sub></em> onto a single degenerate point <em>&mu;</em>, the CLT zooms in on the residual fluctuations by scaling them by <em>&radic;n</em>:
      <div class="formula-box">Z<sub>n</sub> = &radic;n &middot; (X&#772;<sub>n</sub> &minus; &mu;) / &sigma; = (S<sub>n</sub> &minus; n&mu;) / (&sigma;&radic;n)  &rarr;<sup>d</sup>  N(0, 1)</div>
      Here convergence can <em>only</em> be in distribution: <em>Z<sub>n</sub></em> keeps fluctuating as new terms arrive, so it never stabilizes pointwise or in probability, yet its probability law approaches the standard Gaussian bell curve.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>1.4 Why &radic;n Appears Everywhere: Variance Additivity and Independence</h3>
    <p>
      The ubiquitous appearance of <strong class="highlight">&radic;n</strong> across probability and statistics is a direct geometric consequence of how variance behaves under summation. For any sequence <em>X<sub>1</sub>, &hellip;, X<sub>n</sub></em> with finite variances, the variance of the sum <em>S<sub>n</sub> = &sum;<sub>i=1</sub><sup>n</sup> X<sub>i</sub></em> is:
    </p>

    <div class="formula-box">Var(S<sub>n</sub>) = &sum;<sub>i=1</sub><sup>n</sup> Var(X<sub>i</sub>) + 2 &sum;<sub>1 &le; i &lt; j &le; n</sub> Cov(X<sub>i</sub>, X<sub>j</sub>)</div>

    <ul>
      <li><strong class="highlight">The Role of Independence (or Uncorrelatedness):</strong> When the variables are independent (or at least pairwise uncorrelated), every cross-covariance term <em>Cov(X<sub>i</sub>, X<sub>j</sub>)</em> equals zero. Variance therefore becomes strictly <strong class="highlight">additive</strong>: for identically distributed variables with variance <em>&sigma;<sup>2</sup></em>, <em>Var(S<sub>n</sub>) = n&sigma;<sup>2</sup></em>.</li>
      <li><strong class="highlight">From Linear Variance to &radic;n Standard Deviation:</strong> Because standard deviation is the square root of variance (matching the original measurement units of <em>X<sub>i</sub></em>), the typical magnitude of random fluctuations around the mean grows as <em>SD(S<sub>n</sub>) = &radic;(n&sigma;<sup>2</sup>) = &radic;n &middot; &sigma;</em>. Random positive and negative deviations partially cancel each other out, so cumulative noise grows sub-linearly (<em>&radic;n</em>) while the deterministic signal (<em>n&mu;</em>) grows linearly (<em>n</em>).</li>
      <li><strong class="highlight">What Breaks Without Independence:</strong> If observations are strongly positively correlated (in the extreme case, <em>X<sub>1</sub> = X<sub>2</sub> = &hellip; = X<sub>n</sub></em>), all <em>n(n&minus;1)</em> covariance terms survive, making <em>Var(S<sub>n</sub>) = n<sup>2</sup>&sigma;<sup>2</sup></em> and <em>SD(S<sub>n</sub>) = n&sigma;</em>. No cancellation occurs, and the sample mean fails to shrink its variance (<em>Var(X&#772;<sub>n</sub>) = &sigma;<sup>2</sup></em> regardless of <em>n</em>).</li>
      <li><strong class="highlight">Impact on Statistical Inference:</strong> Dividing by <em>n</em> yields the variance of the sample mean: <em>Var(X&#772;<sub>n</sub>) = n&sigma;<sup>2</sup> / n<sup>2</sup> = &sigma;<sup>2</sup> / n</em>, so the <strong class="highlight">Standard Error</strong> is <em>SE(X&#772;<sub>n</sub>) = &sigma; / &radic;n</em>. Consequently:
        <ul>
          <li>Most classical estimators (MLEs, sample means) are <em>&radic;n</em>-consistent, meaning their estimation error decays at rate <em>O<sub>p</sub>(n<sup>&minus;1/2</sup>)</em>.</li>
          <li>Confidence interval widths shrink proportionally to <em>1 / &radic;n</em>: to cut the margin of error in half, a statistician must collect <strong class="highlight">4 times</strong> as many independent observations.</li>
        </ul>
      </li>
    </ul>

