         ====================================================== -->
    <h2>Part 2: Conditional Probability, Bayes' Theorem &amp; Statistical Inference</h2>

    <h3>2.1 Conditional Probability: Probability Theory vs. Statistics</h3>
    <p>
      In axiomatic <strong class="highlight">probability theory</strong>, given two events <em>A</em> and <em>B</em> with <em>P(B) &gt; 0</em>, the conditional probability of <em>A</em> given <em>B</em> is defined as:
    </p>
    <div class="formula-box">P(A | B) = P(A &cap; B) / P(B)</div>
    <p>
      Geometrically, conditioning restricts the sample space <em>&Omega;</em> to the reduced universe <em>B</em> and renormalizes probabilities by dividing by <em>P(B)</em> so the total measure remains 1.
    </p>
    <p>
      In <strong class="highlight">statistics</strong>, conditional probability bridges the forward generative model and inverse inference. A statistical model specifies the conditional distribution of observable data <em>D</em> given an unknown parameter <em>&theta;</em>, written <em>P(D | &theta;)</em>. Statistical inference solves the inverse problem: having observed <em>D</em>, what can we say about <em>&theta;</em>? Frequentist statistics treats <em>&theta;</em> as fixed and evaluates properties of procedures over repeated samples from <em>P(D | &theta;)</em>, whereas <strong class="highlight">Bayesian statistics</strong> treats <em>&theta;</em> itself as a random variable and uses Bayes' Theorem to compute the full conditional distribution <em>P(&theta; | D)</em>.
    </p>

    <h3>2.2 Bayes' Theorem and the Three Core Components</h3>
    <p>
      By symmetry of the intersection <em>P(&theta; &cap; D) = P(D | &theta;) P(&theta;) = P(&theta; | D) P(D)</em>, we immediately obtain <strong class="highlight">Bayes' Theorem</strong>:
    </p>
    <div class="formula-box">P(&theta; | D) = [ P(D | &theta;) &middot; P(&theta;) ] / P(D)   &prop;   L(&theta; | D) &middot; P(&theta;)</div>
    <p>
      where the denominator <em>P(D) = &int; P(D | &theta;) P(&theta;) d&theta;</em> (or <em>&sum;<sub>i</sub> P(D | &theta;<sub>i</sub>) P(&theta;<sub>i</sub>)</em> in the discrete case) is the <strong class="highlight">marginal likelihood (evidence)</strong>, a normalizing constant independent of <em>&theta;</em> ensuring the posterior integrates to 1.
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong class="highlight">Prior Probability <em>P(&theta;)</em>:</strong> Encodes our knowledge, structural constraints, or uncertainty about parameter <em>&theta;</em> <em>before</em> observing the current dataset <em>D</em> (e.g., historical base rates or weakly informative beliefs).</li>
      <li><strong class="highlight">Likelihood Function <em>L(&theta; | D) = P(D | &theta;)</em>:</strong> Measures how plausible the observed data <em>D</em> is as a function of the unknown parameter <em>&theta;</em>. Crucially, while <em>P(D | &theta;)</em> is a probability distribution over <em>D</em> for fixed <em>&theta;</em>, the likelihood is viewed as a function of <em>&theta;</em> for fixed observed <em>D</em> and does not need to integrate to 1 over <em>&theta;</em>.</li>
      <li><strong class="highlight">Posterior Probability <em>P(&theta; | D)</em>:</strong> The updated probability distribution over <em>&theta;</em> <em>after</em> incorporating the evidence from <em>D</em>. It represents an optimal compromise between prior beliefs and the information carried by the data.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>2.3 Why Bayesian Updating Is Central to Statistical Inference</h3>
    <p>
      Bayes' Theorem turns inference into a coherent, recursive learning mechanism. Suppose independent data batches <em>D<sub>1</sub></em> and <em>D<sub>2</sub></em> arrive sequentially. Updating the prior <em>P(&theta;)</em> with <em>D<sub>1</sub></em> yields <em>P(&theta; | D<sub>1</sub>)</em>; using that posterior as the new prior for <em>D<sub>2</sub></em> yields <em>P(&theta; | D<sub>1</sub>, D<sub>2</sub>)</em>—mathematically identical to processing both batches together. This principle ("<strong class="highlight">today's posterior is tomorrow's prior</strong>") makes Bayesian inference naturally suited for streaming telemetry, online anomaly detection, adaptive clinical trials, and decision theory under uncertainty.
    </p>

