          <input type="range" id="bin-n" min="1" max="150" step="1" value="20">
        </div>
        <div class="control-group">
          <label for="bin-k">Observed Breaches (<em>k</em>): <span id="bin-k-val" class="highlight">11</span></label>
          <input type="range" id="bin-k" min="0" max="20" step="1" value="11">
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="results-box" id="beta-output">Calculating...</div>

      <div class="canvas-wrapper">
        <canvas id="bayes-canvas" width="760" height="320"></canvas>
        <div class="legend">
          <div class="legend-item"><span class="swatch" style="background:#00bcd4;"></span> Prior Beta(&alpha;, &beta;)</div>
          <div class="legend-item"><span class="swatch" style="background:#aaaaaa; border-top: 2px dashed #aaaaaa; height:0;"></span> Likelihood (scaled)</div>
          <div class="legend-item"><span class="swatch" style="background:#ff9800;"></span> Posterior Beta(&alpha;+k, &beta;+n&minus;k)</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h3>2.5 Applications, Advantages, Limitations &amp; Influence on Conclusions</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Dimension</th>
          <th>Analysis</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><strong class="highlight">Key Applications</strong></td>
          <td>Spam and malware filtering (Naive Bayes), medical and IDS diagnostics, A/B testing with early stopping, hierarchical models for sparse data, Kalman/particle filters in robotics, and probabilistic machine learning.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong class="highlight">Advantages</strong></td>
          <td>
            &bull; Produces a full probability distribution over <em>&theta;</em>, yielding intuitive <strong class="highlight">credible intervals</strong> (e.g., "a 95% probability that <em>&theta;</em> lies in [a, b]").<br>
            &bull; Naturally incorporates domain knowledge and acts as automatic regularization when data is scarce.<br>
            &bull; Handles sequential data updates seamlessly without invalidating inference.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong class="highlight">Limitations</strong></td>
          <td>
            &bull; <strong class="highlight">Prior sensitivity:</strong> When sample size <em>n</em> is small, poorly chosen or overly dogmatic priors can strongly bias conclusions.<br>
            &bull; <strong class="highlight">Computational cost:</strong> Outside conjugate families (like Beta-Binomial), the evidence integral <em>P(D) = &int; P(D|&theta;)P(&theta;)d&theta;</em> is intractable in high dimensions, requiring numerical approximations such as Markov Chain Monte Carlo (MCMC) or Variational Inference.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong class="highlight">Influence on Statistical Conclusions</strong></td>
          <td>
            The posterior mean in the Beta-Binomial model is a weighted average of the prior mean and the Maximum Likelihood Estimate (<em>k/n</em>):<br>
            <em>E[&theta; | D] = (&alpha; + k) / (&alpha; + &beta; + n) = w &middot; (&alpha; / (&alpha; + &beta;)) + (1 &minus; w) &middot; (k / n)</em>.<br>
            With small <em>n</em>, the prior weight <em>w = (&alpha; + &beta;) / (&alpha; + &beta; + n)</em> pulls extreme sample estimates toward prior expectations (shrinkage). As <em>n &rarr; &infin;</em>, <em>w &rarr; 0</em>: the likelihood overwhelms any non-zero prior, and by the <strong class="highlight">Bernstein&ndash;von Mises Theorem</strong>, the posterior concentrates around the true parameter and becomes asymptotically Gaussian—reconciling Bayesian and frequentist conclusions in the large-sample limit.
          </td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

